        "timestamp": 1774617336,
        "contenuto_esteso": "By Al Jazeera Staff Published On 27 Mar 2026 27 Mar 2026 Qatar’s prime minister has held talks with senior US officials in Washington, DC, amid the ongoing US-Israeli war on Iran and fallout across the Gulf. Sheikh Mohammed bin Abdulrahman bin Jassim Al Thani, who also serves as Qatar’s foreign minister, met US Vice President JD Vance and US Secretary Scott Bessent, Qatar’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs said on Friday. Recommended Stories list of 4 items list 1 of 4 Iran’s closure of the Strait of Hormuz is an international crisis list 2 of 4 US-Iran mediation: What are each side’s demands – and is a deal possible? list 3 of 4 Are Middle East attacks pushing Asia towards an energy crisis? list 4 of 4 Iranian attacks amount to violation of sovereignty, Gulf states tell UN end of list They reviewed ways to strengthen the “close strategic cooperation” between Doha and Washington, “especially the defence partnership in light of the conditions the region is experiencing”, the ministry said. Both sides stressed “ensuring the sustainability of energy supplies and maintaining the continued flow of liquefied natural gas from the State of Qatar to global markets”, in a way that “supports global energy security”, it added. Prime Minister and Minister of Foreign Affairs @MBA_AlThani_ Meets US Vice President, Treasury Secretary Washington, March 27, 2026 HE Prime Minister and Minister of Foreign Affairs Sheikh Mohammed bin Abdulrahman bin Jassim Al-Thani met in Washington with HE Vice President of… — Ministry of Foreign Affairs – Qatar (@MofaQatar_EN) March 27, 2026 Vance hailed the “robust strategic partnership”, praising Qatar’s “active role in promoting regional stability and enhancing global energy security”. The Gulf has been in a state of heightened tension since February 28, when the US-Israeli war on Iran began, which has killed more than 3,000 people across the region, a vast majority of them in Iran and Lebanon. Advertisement Tehran has since launched drone and missile attacks aimed at Israel, as well as Jordan, Iraq, and Gulf states. Iran insists it is targeting US assets in the Gulf, but the region’s leaders have urged Iran to cease attacks as they endanger civilians. Qatar, earlier this month, said Iranian missile attacks on the Ras Laffan Industrial City , the country’s main gas facility, caused “significant damage”. The war has created an unprecedented global energy crisis as Iran has effectively closed off the Strait of Hormuz, through which one-fifth of the world’s oil passes. Meeting with Hegseth On Thursday, Sheikh Mohammed also held a meeting in Washington with US Secretary of Defense Pete Hegseth, the Foreign Ministry said. “The meeting took place in Washington on Thursday and focused on ways to support and develop defence and security collaboration amid regional challenges,” it added. “Both sides stressed the importance of continued coordination and consultation on regional issues to promote security and stability locally and internationally.” On Wednesday, the Qatari Cabinet renewed its condemnation of Iranian attacks on Qatar and its neighbours, calling for an immediate halt."
